## Terraform in one sentence

Terraform is an infrastructure-as-code tool from HashiCorp. It lets you describe infrastructure in configuration files and use that description to provision and manage resources through provider APIs. Resources can include cloud networks, virtual machines, databases, DNS records, and many other services.

Terraform is declarative. You describe the desired result rather than writing a complete script of imperative steps. Terraform compares the desired configuration with information about existing managed resources, builds an execution plan, and attempts to perform the required changes.

## A small example

The following configuration declares an AWS provider and a small compute resource. The exact arguments supported by a resource depend on the provider and its documentation.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 5.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"
}
```

This file is a request for an instance, not a guarantee that the example AMI is valid in every account or region. A real deployment must use an appropriate image and supply credentials safely.

## Why teams use it

Configuration can be reviewed in version control, reused across environments, and changed through an explicit workflow. A plan gives operators a chance to inspect proposed additions, modifications, and deletions before applying them.

Terraform does not make infrastructure automatically safe or universally reproducible. Provider versions, permissions, quotas, regional differences, secrets, and external changes can affect results. Its value comes from making intended infrastructure and proposed changes more visible and repeatable.

## Key distinction

Terraform mainly manages infrastructure resources and their relationships. It is not primarily a general-purpose shell script, a server login tool, or a configuration-management playbook.
